{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on 55039e59 (planner, 2026-10-20). It extends 5a642337; nothing else in it changes.\n\n1. Observation heads join the anchor list. A retained observation names a head of the scope it observes: scope, incarnation, position and hash. When that scope is the register or membership, replay must prove the head, and those journals stay private. So the anchor list also names each such head. verify.ts:1116 already proves an observation's head as a foreign fact. With an anchor, it returns there and leaves the observed value on trust. That is the intended meaning: the report lists the head as taken on the caller's word, and the value with it.\n\n2. One rule from now on. The list names every fact of the register or membership scope that replay must prove for the exported journals, in any of these ways: a copied fact, the head of a retained observation, or any other reference by exact position and hash. A further class of reference of this kind does not need another planner decision. If the dependency is a head with no exact hash in the exported bytes, or a scope other than these two, stop and report it.\n\n3. Same bounds as 5a642337. The list comes only from the exported bytes. It has no live read of the register or membership, and no head that the archiver observes at export time. It names no archived scope. Each item is unique by scope, incarnation, position and hash. The exported bytes already hold each head, so the list adds nothing to the export, and the per-pass budgets bound it.\n\n4. Witnesses: the four target replays reach consistent with Membership 4 and any other observed heads on the list. Without the Membership 4 anchor, replay stops with missing-dependency at Rules entry 2. With its hash changed, replay stops with mismatch. The report lists the observed head among the anchors.\n\n5. The /grow/ page and slice 9's text say that offline replay takes these facts, and the values observed at them, on trust.\n\nNo deployment and no hosted read.","body":{"to":"builder"}}